From 97a8af8303cc50e61753b914475094cc0aa546aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Norris <will@biohack.net>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] [MOS] Legalize and select far pointer values and memory
 accesses

Legalize and select address-space-2 pointers and the byte loads and stores through them.

Legalization: p2 is legal for G_GLOBAL_VALUE, G_FRAME_INDEX and G_BLOCK_ADDR, converts to and from s32 with G_PTRTOINT/G_INTTOPTR, and takes s32 offsets in G_PTR_ADD, which always use the integer carry chain so the add carries into the bank byte. A p2 G_PHI becomes an s32 PHI, with casts placed before each predecessor's terminator and after the block's PHIs. Casting a near pointer to far zero-extends it (bank $00) without the zero-page bias used for direct-page pointers. A byte load or store through a constant or global far address becomes G_LOAD_FAR_ABS/G_STORE_FAR_ABS (absolute long, `lda $xxxxxx`); through a runtime far pointer it becomes G_LOAD_FAR_INDIR/G_STORE_FAR_INDIR (`lda [dp]`). A far memory intrinsic that is not expanded inline fails to legalize: the generic libcall would pass the far pointer to the near runtime and drop the bank byte. The next commit routes it to the far runtime.

When the absolute-long fold rewrites an access, the G_PTR_ADDs that computed its old pointer can be left with only debug uses. The legalizer erases them, and salvageDebugInfo cannot express a G_PTR_ADD, so a DBG_VALUE would name an erased register, which the machine verifier rejects under -g. dropDeadFarAddressDebugUses finds the largest tree of such dead adds from the folded access's pointer, stopping at the first register with a live user, and sets their DBG_VALUE locations to $noreg. The later far folds use the same helper.

Selection: s32 register values use Imag32; a 2 x s16 merge composes an Imag32 with REG_SEQUENCE and an s32 unmerge splits it by word; the far address of a symbol (by address space, including aliases, not by section name) is built from MO_ADDR24_SEG_LO/_SEG_HI/_BANK immediates, which lower to the ADDR24 relocation modifiers; the far access pseudos pin their pointer operand to Imag32 so the cast chain coalesces into the quad `[dp]` reads. Under +mos-a16 the low word of an Imag32 value, as in a far-to-near cast or a truncated ptrtoint, is its sublo16 subregister. Allocation hints skip candidates whose width differs from the copy's other side, and LDCImm treats any nonzero immediate as a set carry. Far code also produces LDImm into imaginary registers, which MOSLateOptimization handled only after llvm-mos#584, an earlier commit of this series.

The 65816 reaches data outside bank DBR only through long addressing; a 16-bit pointer cannot name it. Other MOS CPUs have no long addressing, and the same opcode bytes decode as unrelated instructions there. On those CPUs a far load, store, extending load or memory intrinsic is therefore diagnosed ("far (address space 2) memory access requires 65816 long addressing; the target CPU has none") instead of being selected. A far load or store of any type is custom there, so each access is diagnosed once, before a wide access is split into bytes. Far pointer values need Imag32 quads, which only the 65816 allocates. On other CPUs an instruction that still carries a far pointer value once its accesses are gone (a far pointer that is passed, returned, stored, converted, compared or selected) stops compilation with "far (address space 2) pointer value requires the 65816; the target CPU has no far pointer registers (in function: NAME)", instead of a generic failure to legalize the s32 pieces it would be split into. A far pointer that only addresses diagnosed accesses is erased with them.

Default-mode effect: none for code that compiled before: assembly and objects are identical on all 14 MOS CPUs. Five corpus inputs that use address space 2 fail before and after this commit, with a different error. On every CPU without the 65816 (13 CPUs; four of the inputs on mossweet16) they now stop at a far-specific error: three at the far-access diagnostic and two at the far-value error. On plain mosw65816 they still fail to legalize, now on the s32 merges and unmerges that far pointer values need without +mos-a16.

Tests: far-addressing.ll checks absolute-long and `[dp]` loads and stores in assembly and object bytes ($af/$8f/$a7/$87), and that a near symbol placed in a far section stays near. far-phi.ll checks the p2 PHI legalization and a `[dp]` load in the emitted loop. far-ptr-arg-exhaustion.ll adds full compilation of its call shapes in +mos-a16 and +mos-a16,+mos-xy16. far-quad-spill-call.ll compiles four far pointers live across a call, which spills a quad, at O0 and O2 in both modes. far-access-non-65816.ll checks one diagnostic per access, including an i32 load and a far memset, on mos6502, mos65c02 and mosspc700, and that the byte accesses compile on mosw65816. It also checks the far-value error for passing a far pointer (mos6502 at O2, mosspc700 at O0), converting a near pointer to far (mos65c02 at O0) and storing a far pointer (mos6502), and that passing one compiles on mosw65816. far-ptr-trunc.ll checks the far-to-near cast and truncated ptrtoint at O0 and O2. far-fold-debug.ll compiles a -g far global plus a constant verifier-clean in plain mosw65816, +mos-a16 and +mos-a16,+mos-xy16 at O0 and O2, and checks that the dead address's DBG_VALUE is $noreg after the legalizer.

Validate: `build/bin/llvm-lit -v llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-addressing.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-phi.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-ptr-arg-exhaustion.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-quad-spill-call.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-access-non-65816.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-ptr-trunc.ll llvm/test/CodeGen/MOS/far-fold-debug.ll`.

